  <section>
    <h2 class="section-title">How Much Does YouTube Pay?</h2>
    <p>YouTube pays creators <strong>55% of the advertising revenue</strong> their videos generate &mdash; YouTube keeps the other 45%. Your share is measured as <strong>RPM (revenue per 1,000 views)</strong>: the amount you actually earn per thousand views after YouTube&rsquo;s cut.</p>
    <p>Because advertisers bid different amounts for different audiences, RPM varies widely. In higher-ad-spend countries like the United States, United Kingdom, Canada and Australia, long-form RPMs of <strong>$3&ndash;$8</strong> are typical; in many other regions RPMs sit under $1. Niche matters too &mdash; finance, business and software content attracts premium advertisers, while broad entertainment pays less per view.</p>
    <p>Shorts are a separate system: all Shorts ad revenue goes into one shared pool that is divided among creators by their share of total Shorts views. That is why Shorts RPM is far lower &mdash; usually around <strong>$0.03&ndash;$0.10</strong>.</p>
  </section>

